// ============================================================================
//  config/alloy/config.alloy
//  Grafana Alloy v1.18.1 - collect logs from ALL Docker containers on the host
// ============================================================================
//
//  PIPELINE
//    discovery.docker    -> polls the Docker API for running containers
//    discovery.relabel   -> (a) filters which containers get a tailer
//                           (b) exports the rules that build the final labels
//    loki.source.docker  -> one tailer per container, applies (b) per entry
//    loki.process        -> age guard, rate guard, structured metadata,
//                           hard label allow-list
//    loki.write          -> pushes to http://loki:3100 on the compose network
//
//  CONTRACT WITH docker-compose.yml (this file does not work without it):
//    1. /var/run/docker.sock mounted into the alloy container.
//    2. --storage.path=/var/lib/alloy/data backed by the `alloy-data` volume.
//       Positions live at
//         /var/lib/alloy/data/loki.source.docker.containers/positions.yml
//       If that path is not persisted, every ALLOY restart re-reads each
//       container from the beginning.
//       CAVEAT: this does NOT cover CONTAINER restarts. When a container stops,
//       its discovery target disappears, the tailer stops and DELETES its
//       positions entry. A `docker restart` therefore re-reads that container's
//       whole retained log. Bounded by the 24h age drop below and by daemon-level
//       json-file rotation (item 6).
//    3. Alloy needs read access to the socket - it runs as root in the image.
//       Socket access is root-equivalent on the host; `:ro` is NOT a boundary.
//    4. --server.http.listen-addr=0.0.0.0:12345 inside the container, published
//       as ports: ["127.0.0.1:12345:12345"]. The UI is UNAUTHENTICATED and
//       renders every argument value, so loopback only.
//    5. Loki must resolve as `loki` on port 3100 on the compose network.
//    6. The host daemon MUST set json-file rotation in /etc/docker/daemon.json
//       (max-size 50m / max-file 3, then `sudo systemctl restart docker`).
//       Loki's 30d retention does NOT bound /var/lib/docker/containers/*/*.log:
//       Alloy reads through the API and never truncates it. Without rotation the
//       host disk fills and takes the Docker daemon - and this whole stack -
//       down with it. Corollary: 150MB/container is Alloy's catch-up budget if
//       it stalls; data rotated away before Alloy reads it is lost.
//
//  KNOWN GAPS (no config closes these - verify coverage after deploy):
//    * Discovery is a POLL, not an event stream. A container whose entire
//      lifetime is shorter than refresh_interval is NEVER discovered and its
//      logs are lost silently: `docker compose run --rm` one-offs, init/migration
//      jobs, and crash-looping containers that exit in under 2s.
//    * A container with NO attached network (network_mode: none, or an
//      unresolvable network_mode:container:<id>) produces zero discovery targets
//      and is silently never tailed. Isolate with an `internal: true` network
//      instead of network_mode: none.
//    * Only json-file, local and journald log drivers are natively readable
//      through the API. Others work only via Docker's dual-logging cache, and
//      `logging: {driver: none}` makes a container permanently invisible AND
//      produces a "could not fetch logs" error on every discovery tick. Never
//      set driver: none on this host; label such containers logging.exclude=true.
//    Verify actual coverage:
//      docker ps --format '{{.Names}}' | sort
//      vs. `count by (container) (count_over_time({job="docker"}[1h]))` in Grafana
// ============================================================================


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Alloy's own logging. `write_to` is deliberately NOT set: piping Alloy's
// internal logs into this same pipeline is the classic amplification loop
// (Loki down -> loki.write logs a retry error -> that error is ingested ->
// more backlog -> more errors). Reach them via `docker logs logging-alloy` or
// the UI on 127.0.0.1:12345.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
logging {
  level  = "info"
  format = "logfmt"
}


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1. DISCOVERY - every running container, refreshed on a timer.
//
// This POLLS GET /containers/json (plus a network list) every refresh_interval;
// there is no Docker event stream anywhere in this path. Each poll re-exports
// .targets and Alloy's graph starts a tailer for each newly-seen container ID
// and stops the tailer for any that disappeared. So a container started at T is
// tailed by roughly T+2s with no config edit and no Alloy restart.
//
// 2s halves the total-loss window for short-lived containers versus the 5s that
// Grafana's own Docker integration ships, at a cost of one container-list plus
// one network-list API call every 2s - noise on a 16-core host. It cannot be
// closed at any value; see KNOWN GAPS above. Raise to 15-30s only if you ever
// run hundreds of containers.
//
// Docker's default filter lists RUNNING containers only, which is what we want.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
discovery.docker "containers" {
  host             = "unix:///var/run/docker.sock"
  refresh_interval = "2s"
}


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2a. TARGET FILTERING - decide WHICH containers get a tailer.
//
// Runs at discovery time; a dropped target never gets a tailer at all, which is
// strictly cheaper than filtering log lines later. `drop` only removes targets -
// every __meta_docker_* label (including the container ID the tailer needs)
// survives untouched into .output.
//
// Default policy is ALL containers. These are the only exclusions.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
discovery.relabel "containers" {
  targets = discovery.docker.containers.targets

  // Generic opt-out. Any container labelled logging.exclude=true is skipped.
  // Docker label dots are sanitised to underscores in the meta-label name.
  // Carried by all three stack containers in docker-compose.yml (alloy, loki and
  // grafana); also the escape hatch for a container using a log driver the API
  // cannot read back, or one so chatty it is not worth indexing.
  rule {
    source_labels = ["__meta_docker_container_label_logging_exclude"]
    regex         = "true"
    action        = "drop"
  }

  // Belt and braces for THIS stack's own alloy and loki containers, in case the
  // label above is ever dropped from the compose file. Grafana is deliberately
  // NOT in this rule: it is excluded for noise, not safety, so losing its label
  // costs you a chattier index - not a feedback loop - and a second mechanism
  // would only make that preference harder to reverse.
  //   * alloy: tailing itself feeds its own retry errors back into itself.
  //   * loki:  every ingestion rejection (429 rate limit, 400 too-old, disk
  //            full) writes an error line to Loki's stdout. Shipping that line
  //            back into Loki produces another rejection and another line - a
  //            loop that engages exactly when Loki is already degraded.
  // Matched on project AND service (joined by "/") so a container named "loki"
  // in somebody else's compose stack is still collected normally.
  rule {
    source_labels = [
      "__meta_docker_container_label_com_docker_compose_project",
      "__meta_docker_container_label_com_docker_compose_service",
    ]
    separator = "/"
    regex     = "logging/(alloy|loki)"
    action    = "drop"
  }
}


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2b. LABEL CONSTRUCTION - exported purely for its `.rules`.
//
// `targets = []` is the documented idiom: this component relabels nothing, it
// exists so loki.source.docker can borrow its rule list.
//
// The rules must live HERE and not in the block above because
// __meta_docker_container_log_stream does not exist at discovery time - the
// tailer sets it per log entry, immediately before these rules run.
//
// Order inside the tailer: target labels (all __meta_*) -> set
// __meta_docker_container_log_stream -> apply these rules -> DROP every label
// whose name starts with "__". So __meta_* can never leak into Loki.
//
// Regexes are fully anchored by the relabeller (^(?:...)$). A rule whose regex
// does not match is a no-op, which is what makes the `(.+)` fallback chains
// below work: they simply do nothing for containers that lack the source label.
//
// CONTRACT: this label set is what config/grafana/dashboards/container-logs.json
// queries. Change one, change the other.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
discovery.relabel "container_labels" {
  targets = []

  // --- job ----------------------------------------------------------------
  // Constant, so zero cardinality, and a single cheap entry point for
  // "show me everything": {job="docker"}.
  rule {
    target_label = "job"
    replacement  = "docker"
  }

  // --- container ----------------------------------------------------------
  // The primary identity label. Docker reports names with a leading slash
  // ("/logging-loki"); the capture group strips it. `/?` makes the rule
  // idempotent. One value per container on the host - this is the finest-grained
  // label kept and therefore the one that sets the stream count.
  rule {
    source_labels = ["__meta_docker_container_name"]
    regex         = "/?(.*)"
    target_label  = "container"
  }

  // --- compose_project ----------------------------------------------------
  // Set only for compose-managed containers. `(.+)` does not match the empty
  // string, so plain `docker run` containers get no label rather than an empty
  // one. (The dashboard's variables use allValue ".*" precisely so that streams
  // WITHOUT this label are still matched by "All".)
  rule {
    source_labels = ["__meta_docker_container_label_com_docker_compose_project"]
    regex         = "(.+)"
    target_label  = "compose_project"
  }

  // --- compose_service ----------------------------------------------------
  // Coarser than `container` (all replicas share it) and a pure function of the
  // container, so it adds no streams - only a few bytes per existing stream.
  rule {
    source_labels = ["__meta_docker_container_label_com_docker_compose_service"]
    regex         = "(.+)"
    target_label  = "compose_service"
  }

  // --- service_name -------------------------------------------------------
  // Loki 3.x stamps service_name="unknown_service" on any stream lacking a
  // recognised service label, and Grafana Logs Drilldown keys off it. Two rules
  // form a fallback chain: rule 1 always sets the container name, rule 2
  // overwrites with the compose service when one exists. Pure function of labels
  // already kept => zero extra streams.
  rule {
    source_labels = ["__meta_docker_container_name"]
    regex         = "/?(.*)"
    target_label  = "service_name"
  }
  rule {
    source_labels = ["__meta_docker_container_label_com_docker_compose_service"]
    regex         = "(.+)"
    target_label  = "service_name"
  }

  // --- stream -------------------------------------------------------------
  // Exactly two values. Doubles the stream count in the worst case (50
  // containers -> 100 streams), which is trivial, and "only stderr" is a
  // first-class query.
  rule {
    source_labels = ["__meta_docker_container_log_stream"]
    regex         = "(.+)"
    target_label  = "stream"
  }

  // --- container_id (TEMPORARY - becomes structured metadata) --------------
  // Truncated to the 12 chars `docker ps` shows. Created as a label ONLY so
  // loki.process can read it out of the extracted map; stage.label_keep below
  // strips it from the index. It must never reach Loki as a label - it changes
  // on every container recreation and would orphan a stream on every deploy.
  // Why it earns its place: after a recreate, `container` and `compose_service`
  // are identical across old and new; the short ID is the only thing that says
  // which incarnation produced a line.
  rule {
    source_labels = ["__meta_docker_container_id"]
    regex         = "(.{12}).*"
    target_label  = "container_id"
  }

  // --- image (TEMPORARY - becomes structured metadata) ---------------------
  // BEST EFFORT, AND KNOWN INCOMPLETE. Alloy's Docker discovery delegates to
  // Prometheus's moby SD, whose common labels are exactly container id, name and
  // network_mode plus the sanitised container labels - there is no
  // __meta_docker_container_image, so the image cannot be read directly.
  //
  // Fallback chain, worst source first (later rules overwrite earlier ones):
  //   1. com.docker.compose.image  - stamped by Compose on every container it
  //      creates, but it is an image DIGEST (sha256:...), not a readable tag.
  //   2/3. OCI image labels, which Docker copies from the image onto the
  //      container. Many images set them; many do not.
  //   4. logging.image - stamp it yourself on containers you control:
  //        labels: ["logging.image=myrepo/myapp:1.2.3"]
  //
  // Carried as STRUCTURED METADATA, never as an index label: an image tag
  // changes on every deploy, so an `image` LABEL would mint a brand-new stream
  // per container per release.
  // RESIDUAL GAP: a plain `docker run` container from an image with no OCI
  // labels and no logging.image label gets no image value at all.
  rule {
    source_labels = ["__meta_docker_container_label_com_docker_compose_image"]
    regex         = "(.+)"
    target_label  = "image"
  }
  rule {
    source_labels = ["__meta_docker_container_label_org_opencontainers_image_version"]
    regex         = "(.+)"
    target_label  = "image"
  }
  rule {
    source_labels = ["__meta_docker_container_label_org_opencontainers_image_ref_name"]
    regex         = "(.+)"
    target_label  = "image"
  }
  rule {
    source_labels = ["__meta_docker_container_label_logging_image"]
    regex         = "(.+)"
    target_label  = "image"
  }
}


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3. TAILING - one reader per discovered container.
//
// Reads via the Docker API, so the entry timestamp comes from Docker rather
// than being guessed from the line. REQUIRES a readable log driver - see the
// KNOWN GAPS block at the top of this file.
//
// Read offsets are checkpointed to positions.yml under this component's data
// directory, so a restart resumes each container NEAR where it left off.
// Offsets are stored at SECOND granularity and Docker's `since` is inclusive of
// that whole second, so an Alloy restart replays up to one second of
// already-ingested lines per container. Expect a small, bounded number of
// duplicates around every restart; this is not a bug to chase.
//
// FIRST BOOT / DELETED alloy-data VOLUME: positions.yml is empty, the API is
// called with since=0 (which is "no time filter", not "tail from now"), and
// every running container's FULL retained history is replayed with its ORIGINAL
// timestamps. loki.source.docker has no tail-from-end option. The stage.drop
// age guard below is what bounds that burst.
//
// `refresh_interval` is left at its default here: it only sets the HTTP client
// timeout when the daemon is reached over http/https and is unused for a unix
// socket. New-container pickup is governed solely by discovery.docker above.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
loki.source.docker "containers" {
  host          = "unix:///var/run/docker.sock"
  targets       = discovery.relabel.containers.output
  relabel_rules = discovery.relabel.container_labels.rules
  forward_to    = [loki.process.containers.receiver]
}


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 4. PROCESSING - age guard, rate guard, structured metadata, cardinality guard.
//
// loki.process seeds its extracted-data map with the entry's labels before any
// stage runs, which is what lets container_id and image cross from label ->
// structured metadata here.
//
// Drop criteria INSIDE a single stage.drop block are ANDed, so each independent
// reason needs its own block.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
loki.process "containers" {
  forward_to = [loki.write.local.receiver]

  // Bounds the first-boot backfill and the re-read after any `docker restart`.
  // Without it, a clean rebuild replays months of history from every running
  // container in one burst. 24h is deliberately far below Loki's
  // reject_old_samples_max_age (720h), so nothing Alloy ships is ever rejected
  // as too old - widen this if you genuinely want more history back-filled.
  stage.drop {
    older_than          = "24h"
    drop_counter_reason = "older_than_24h"
  }

  // Blank lines carry no information and are common padding in container output.
  stage.drop {
    expression          = `^\s*$`
    drop_counter_reason = "empty_line"
  }

  // Per-container token bucket. loki.process -> loki.write is a SINGLE shared
  // pipeline: without this, one container in a crash-restart loop dumping stack
  // traces backpressures and starves every other container's logs - a total
  // observability outage caused by one noisy neighbour, at exactly the moment
  // you need everyone else's logs. by_label_name REQUIRES drop = true, which is
  // the point: shed the offender's excess instead of blocking the pipeline.
  // Watch loki_process_dropped_lines_total{reason="rate_limit"}.
  // max_distinct_labels: Alloy silently clamps anything below 10000 up to 10000
  // and logs "max_distinct_labels was adjusted up to the minimal reasonable
  // value" on every start. Written at the effective value so the file matches
  // what actually runs. It caps how many per-container buckets are tracked; far
  // above any realistic container count on this host.
  stage.limit {
    rate                = 1000
    burst               = 5000
    by_label_name       = "container"
    drop                = true
    max_distinct_labels = 10000
  }

  // Stored per entry inside the chunk, never in the stream index, so these cost
  // no cardinality while remaining filterable:
  //   {job="docker"} | container_id="a1b2c3d4e5f6"
  // An empty value means "use the extracted key with the same name".
  stage.structured_metadata {
    values = {
      container_id = "",
      image        = "",
    }
  }

  // The cardinality backstop. Anything not on this list - including the
  // container_id and image labels consumed above, and anything a future edit
  // might accidentally introduce - is removed before it can reach the index.
  //
  // Final index label set, and nothing else, ever. Must match the label names
  // used by config/grafana/dashboards/container-logs.json.
  stage.label_keep {
    values = [
      "job",
      "container",
      "compose_project",
      "compose_service",
      "service_name",
      "stream",
    ]
  }
}


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 5. WRITE - to Loki over the compose network.
//
// `loki` is the compose service name; Docker's embedded DNS resolves it on the
// `logging` network. No tenant_id: Loki runs single-tenant with
// auth_enabled=false and assigns the "fake" tenant itself.
//
// DURABILITY: loki.write also has a `wal` block that would persist entries to
// disk before pushing. It is NOT enabled here because it is an EXPERIMENTAL
// feature that requires running the whole process with
// --stability.level=experimental. Instead the retry budget below is widened.
// Consequence, stated plainly: a Loki outage longer than roughly one hour
// discards the in-flight batch PERMANENTLY (the tailer has already advanced its
// position, so those lines are never re-read). Alert on
// loki_write_dropped_entries_total. To opt into the WAL instead, add
// --stability.level=experimental to the alloy command in docker-compose.yml and
// a `wal { enabled = true, max_segment_age = "1h" }` block here, and raise the
// alloy stop_grace_period above the 30s default drain_timeout.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
loki.write "local" {
  endpoint {
    url = "http://loki:3100/loki/api/v1/push"

    // Batching. Stated explicitly because these are the knobs you reach for
    // first if ingest ever falls behind.
    batch_wait     = "1s"
    batch_size     = "1MiB"
    remote_timeout = "10s"

    // Retry budget, stated explicitly. The defaults (10 retries, 500ms -> 5m)
    // give only ~8.5 MINUTES before the in-flight batch is dropped for good.
    // 20 retries extends that to roughly an hour, which covers a slow Loki
    // restart, a long compaction pass or an OOM-kill loop. Raising this is cheap
    // because the client stops draining its input while retrying, so unread data
    // stays in Docker's json files rather than in Alloy's memory - provided
    // those files are rotation-bounded but not tiny (50m x 3, see contract 6).
    // 4xx responses are NOT retried at any setting.
    min_backoff_period  = "500ms"
    max_backoff_period  = "5m"
    max_backoff_retries = 20
  }
}
